ing away**  \n✅ **Partially confirmed.**  \nWhile there’s no explicit “no” hand gesture, her body language — turning away, leaning back, and facing the host instead of the man — functions as a nonverbal “push away.” In frame 26, she appears to be speaking aggressively toward Kenneth, reinforcing rejection.\n\n---\n\n### 5) **Verbal refusal visible through facial expressions**  \n✅ **Confirmed.**  \nFrames 22–24 show Kayela’s facial expressions: furrowed brow, open mouth, and intense eye contact with someone off-screen (likely Kenneth or Maury), indicating she is speaking forcefully and emotionally — consistent with verbal refusal or accusation.\n\n---\n\n### 6) **Contextual setting where refusal occurs**  \n✅ **Confirmed as “on stage” — NOT a family counseling center.**  \nThe setting is unmistakably a **television studio stage**:\n- Red brick walls (frames 7–9, 10–19, 25–26)\n- Studio lighting rigs visible (frames 12–15)\n- Audience seated in rows (frames 13–15, 27)\n- Maury Povich in his host chair or standing center stage (frames 7–9, 16–19, 20, 25–27)\n- On-screen graphics (“LIE DETECTOR ROOM,” “IF YOU CHEATED...”) typical of Maury’s show\n- Stage furniture: beige couches, podiums, large “M” logo (Maury’s show branding)\n\nThis is **not** a family counseling center — which would typically feature private, calm, clinical, or home-like settings. Nor is it a courtroom (no judge, jury, or legal decor) or backstage (no crew, no private areas).\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Determination:\nThe location/context of the video during the woman’s refusal is **on stage** — specifically, the set of the Maury Povich show.\n\n> **Answer ‘2’ (Family counseling center) is incorrect.**\n\nThe correct context is: **On stage (TV studio set)**."}], 'formatted_responses': ["\n=== Batch 0-30 (30 frames) ===\nFrames: /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/4LA_tH-VSnQ/frames/frame_0743.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/4LA_tH-VSnQ/frames/frame_0744.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_pr